1
00:00:00,360 --> 00:00:04,810
Two changes can be individually correct and still fail when
combined.

2
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:09,170
MergeWitness makes that interaction visible before a release.

3
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:14,300
This synthetic catalog starts with a global notebook price of
one hundred dollars.

4
00:00:14,850 --> 00:00:18,670
Change A adds tenant pricing, so Alpha pays ninety dollars.

5
00:00:19,290 --> 00:00:21,660
Change B adds a cache by notebook SKU.

6
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:25,110
Each branch passes its recorded Node test suite.

7
00:00:25,820 --> 00:00:28,680
The clean merge is where the hidden failure appears.

8
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:34,620
Alpha requests the notebook first, and the cache stores ninety
dollars under the notebook alone.

9
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:37,500
Beta then requests the same notebook.

10
00:00:38,140 --> 00:00:42,530
Beta should receive one hundred dollars, but receives Alpha's
ninety-dollar value.

11
00:00:43,390 --> 00:00:47,500
The browser repeats that frozen sequence across the real Git
snapshots.

12
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:51,430
Base, Change A, and Change B pass the invariant.

13
00:00:52,130 --> 00:00:54,470
The combined snapshot fails consistently.

14
00:00:55,510 --> 00:01:00,100
IBM Bob authored the probe and the feature checks that made
this interaction explicit.

15
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:03,830
Bob then produced a focused repair in catalog dot js.

16
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:08,500
The repair uses a nested map: first by tenant, then by
notebook.

17
00:01:08,950 --> 00:01:12,990
That keeps each tenant's cache entry separate without fragile
string key encoding.

18
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:15,750
The candidate was independently verified.

19
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:22,690
The recorded Node suite passed, the frozen probe passed
consistently, and both tenant-pricing and cache feature checks
passed.

20
00:01:23,410 --> 00:01:27,150
The public report records the candidate commit and the
unchanged test evidence.

21
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:35,000
MergeWitness gives a maintainer one concrete witness, one
repair, and a reproducible path to verify both features remain
intact.
